{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact 18d69cda91e7bde0ca5533c5c7edb7e0e73ac190 for request #9796 (git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d29c09faef7e54cdf1199eb86344fa7062d38177). I inspected all 21 effective artifacts at this head, with plans/README.md as the primary. The earlier projection leaks are repaired, but one P2 remains in the at-rest upgrade.\n\nP2 — Run the upgrade before founding as well. RoomCore.scrubErrors and nextAlarm (packages/room/src/core.ts:1853 and :1976 at this head) correctly run and schedule batches for a founded Room. However, Room.work (:203), schedule (:208) and recover (:253) in packages/room/src/room.ts select settleFounding/foundingDue whenever genesis is not sealed. Those paths neither run scrubErrors nor consider error_scrub. A failed public founding can finish all remote cleanup while remaining unfounded; foundingDue then returns null. Its retained, completed artroom_ws_duty rows can still contain provider text from version 1. Reopening runs migration 2 and stores error_scrub, but no alarm is scheduled to scrub it. Even a manually triggered alarm only settles founding and leaves the cursor and old error unchanged. The safe read guards prevent the earlier projection leaks; they do not satisfy the promised at-rest repair of every row.\n\nIndependent real-DO control: fail creation-token revocation during public founding, run the normal alarm to settle the debt without sealing genesis, seed a completed duty's legacy error, set schema version 1, delete the alarm, abort and reopen. The object is still unfounded, foundingDue is null and migration 2's cursor exists. Production alarm attempts leave the synthetic legacy credential in the terminal row. The named runtime assertion is “terminal legacy founding error remains without any cleanup alarm”; no compile or load failure was counted. Reproduction source and full log: /tmp/artroom-checker-error-sinks-v2-founding-control.test.ts and /tmp/artroom-checker-error-sinks-v2-founding-control.log.\n\nRepair on the same request and promise: schedule and run the bounded local scrub independently of founded state, through fresh-object recovery and the normal alarm path. Preserve the earlier founding wake, all remote ownership/cleanup rules and cursor transaction; do not invoke founded-only steps before genesis or run the entire upgrade synchronously at open. Add real-DO controls for both unfinished remote founding debt and completed terminal founding rows, with no founder retry, no direct scrubErrors call, multiple batches and a restart between them. The cursor must drain and disappear, later runs must write nothing, and a new empty unfounded room must remain quiet. Mutate the pre-founding scheduling and execution guards separately.\n\nArchitecture: Artroom's Git publishers/landing and Room storage/alarm composition remain application implementation in ordinary Git (layer 1), coordinated through Workroom application/interpreter and projections (layers 5–7) as described by gitseq/docs/reference/architecture.md. This head changes none of Gitseq's kernel, live nexus or host-binding contracts (layers 2–4), and invents no signed-history fact. It implements the existing safe-error requirement rather than changing protocol meaning. The new storage migration and keyed cursor preserve retained owner state and signed history. The P2 is a missing host recovery route into that otherwise correct local upgrade.\n\nSecurity: provider messages, repository names and arbitrary error codes are untrusted. The closed safe-text language, whole-value replacement and immediate landing/workspace/job read guards answer the earlier confidentiality leaks; new sink writes and known publication codes remain guarded. Both earlier checker reopen controls now pass. Unknown mints, held ownership, token IDs, expiry, retry times, attempts and signed acts are preserved. The upgrade's dynamically named tables/columns are trusted internal descriptors, with bounded indexed paging and a transaction spanning rows and cursor. Its remaining confidentiality defect is the retained legacy provider text in an unfounded store.\n\nThe durable authority chain was checked again: planner's effective #9796 request commissions this sweep, builder's effective promise #9876 binds the artifact set, its parent mint-B request #9475 is satisfied by the sealed landing, and the broader #8651 commission limited the design to contract and lane edits followed by separate implementation requests. Design approval #9463 is ratified and its sealed receipt #9465 adopted the decision; the current merged note #9467 stands. Planner's operator foundation #1 and current roster provide request authority. All six implementing/report commits after base carry the full #9796 Rests-On reference. No review or implementation request grants live-provider/deployment authority.\n\nSimplification: one validator and one table-driven upgrade are appropriate. Reuse their bounded step for both host states, without duplicating cleanup logic or opening the grammar to free text. No separate schema rewrite, history rewrite or token inventory sweep is needed. The head overlaps mint C at index.ts/client.ts/jobs.ts; any later semantic merge needs its own exact-head evidence.\n\nValidation: clean disposable checkouts /tmp/artroom-checker-error-sinks-v2 and ...-guards at the exact head; npm ci, root typecheck and root npm test exit 0 (2,277 passed, one skipped); separate Git Workers gate 11 passed; Room dry-run bundle and scratch Git harness typecheck exit 0. All 24 new advertised mutants and 21 retained sink mutants were independently applied and produced named runtime assertion failures at this head. Two supplementary mutants strengthen repository-name rejection while retaining the valid phrase, and remove the new scrub transaction: both runtime-red. Logs and results are under /tmp/artroom-checker-error-sinks-v2-mutants; all source restored. Restored suites: 19 Node and 45 Room tests pass. Two additional real-DO controls pass: production alarms drain 501 terminal rows in batches of 500 and 1 and then write nothing; an injected cursor-write failure rolls back row changes with the cursor (the transaction mutant fails this assertion). Their source/logs are ...-batch-control.test.ts and ...-batch-control-2.log. The first batch-control attempt used an aborted stale stub and was corrected; that harness failure is not a product finding or a red proof. Canonical main remains clean at 574568b2c5dfebb1c3b2a30c8afc9b9d7c3719a3. No canonical source edit, deployment, live credential or real provider action occurred.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0b2afe536db52412414f68921bd7108bcfea335f","binding":"assigned","head":"18d69cda91e7bde0ca5533c5c7edb7e0e73ac190","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0838c28719c6b279877d751ca566a5f58a92dcce\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:60d5c3a62d4ea5f8bea1cdb923381a13b10d1d0f\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b84aead90f9caeb8871040f55497e4345ec3f218","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}